course: dictionary
lesson: 1824
title: Coal: a rock and a glowing piece
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Read the words around them to find out which one fits. In «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air», coal is the black rock we burn for fuel, because the sentence is about fuel and air.
explain: In «Then a coal slipped, the fire crackled and sent up a spurt of flame», coal means one glowing piece in the fire. The crackling and the flame are the clues. Try the swap check: does "one burning piece" fit the sentence? Yes, so that is the meaning.
explain: Look at the phrases too. A coal mine and a coal miner are about the rock dug from the ground. Hot coals are about the glowing pieces. Words that go with a word often show you which meaning it has.
fact: Coal is a hard black rock that is burned as fuel to give heat or make electricity.
fact: A coal is one burning or glowing piece of coal or wood in a fire.
fact: «Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels formed over millions of years from ancient plants and animals.»
fact: Burning coal adds carbon dioxide to the air, which warms the planet, so many countries now use less coal.
fact: When coal means the rock, it has no plural, but you can say a coal for one glowing piece.
exercise: In «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air», what is coal? | *A hard black rock that is burned as fuel | One glowing piece in a fire | A small brown animal | A type of cloud
exercise: In «Then a coal slipped, the fire crackled and sent up a spurt of flame», what is a coal? | A bird that sings at night | A pet kept in a box | A kind of wind | *One glowing piece of burning coal or wood
exercise: Which phrase shows that coal is dug out of the ground? | a glowing coal | hot coals | *coal mine
test: «The fire was low, so he poked one red-hot coal back into the grate.» What does coal mean here? | A fossil from a dinosaur | A hard black rock dug from a mine | A kind of fuel gas | *One burning piece in the fire
test: Why do many countries now use less coal? | *Burning it adds carbon dioxide, which warms the planet | It costs nothing to mine | It makes the air sweet and clean | It is too soft to burn
exam: What does the phrase «carry coals to Newcastle» mean? | Dig a very deep hole | *Bring something to a place where there is already plenty of it | Walk a long way with a heavy load | Travel north by train
